---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import GuideTable from '../../components/GuideTable.astro';
import { bySlug, monthRanges, MONTHS } from '../../lib/data';

const cusco = bySlug.get('cusco')!;
const wettest = [...cusco.months].sort((a, b) => b.rain - a.rain)[0];
const driest = [...cusco.months].sort((a, b) => a.rain - b.rain)[0];
---

<Guide slug="machu-picchu" photo="cusco">
  <p>
    The best time to visit Machu Picchu is the <strong>dry season, from May to September</strong>, when mornings are clear and
    the trails are firm. The shoulder months of <strong>April and October</strong> are a good compromise: still mostly dry,
    greener and less crowded. The rainy season runs from November to March, peaking in January and February, and the
    <strong>Inca Trail closes every February</strong> for maintenance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Machu Picchu sits at about 2,430 m, lower and warmer than Cusco (3,400 m), where most trips start. Our climate figures are
    for Cusco: the wettest month there is {wettest.name} (about {wettest.rain} mm), the driest {driest.name}
    ({driest.rain} mm). Our weather ratings put the best months at {monthRanges(cusco.bestMonths)}.
  </p>

  <h2>Cusco and the Sacred Valley by month</h2>
  <GuideTable caption="Cusco weather rating by month" months={MONTHS.map((_, i) => i)} rows={[{ slug: 'cusco', label: 'Cusco & Machu Picchu' }, { slug: 'lima' }]} />
  <GuideTable caption="Cusco temperatures by month" months={MONTHS.map((_, i) => i)} show="temp" rows={[{ slug: 'cusco', label: 'Cusco (high / low)' }]} />

  <h2>Dry season vs rainy season</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>May–September (dry):</b> sunny days and cold nights (often near freezing in Cusco in June–July). It’s the busiest time, especially in June–August and around Inti Raymi on 24 June.</li>
    <li><b>April and October (shoulder):</b> the sweet spot for many travellers — fewer people, green terraces and mostly good weather.</li>
    <li><b>November–March (rainy):</b> afternoon downpours, clouds over the ruins and occasional landslides on the railway. The site stays open (except the Inca Trail in February), it’s lush and much quieter.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tickets, circuits and visitor limits</h2>
  <p>
    Entry is by timed ticket for a set circuit (route) through the site, sold through the Ministry of Culture’s official
    website. Daily numbers are capped — around 4,500 visitors on most days in 2026, rising to about 5,600 on peak dates from
    late June to early November. Tickets for the most popular circuits, and for the add-on hikes up Huayna Picchu or Machu
    Picchu Mountain, sell out weeks or months ahead in the dry season; a limited number are sold in person in Aguas Calientes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classic four-day <b>Inca Trail</b> is limited to 500 people a day including porters and guides, can only be booked
    through licensed operators, and often sells out six months or more ahead for May–August. Alternatives like the Salkantay
    and Lares treks don’t need permits.
  </p>

  <h2>Tips for visiting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Acclimatise first.</b> Spend a night or two in the lower Sacred Valley (about 2,800 m) before Cusco to ease altitude sickness.</li>
    <li><b>Go early or late.</b> The first entry slots have the best chance of clouds lifting dramatically; afternoons can be quieter.</li>
    <li><b>Book the train early.</b> Trains from Cusco/Ollantaytambo to Aguas Calientes (PeruRail and Inca Rail) fill up in the dry season.</li>
    <li><b>Pack layers and rain gear</b> in any season — the weather changes quickly in the mountains.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>See also: <a href="/best-time-to-visit-peru/">best time to visit Peru</a> and <a href="/destinations/cusco/">Cusco month by month</a>.</p>
</Guide>
